We introduce a novel two-dimensional optical drag effect, termed "anomalous optical drag," which arises from the interaction between a two-dimensional electron gas and a circularly polarized laser. This phenomenon is driven by the non-adiabatic coupling between Landau levels. We demonstrate that this effect can be observed through the circular photogalvanic current and the Hall conductivity. Furthermore, we show that the anomalous optical drag can be tuned by adjusting the inter-landau level spacing. This capability offers a new avenue for investigating topological states in two-dimensional electron gases. Additionally, we provide experimental guidelines for realizing this effect in graphene. Finally, we discuss the potential for observing this effect in other two-dimensional electron gases, such as those found in topological insulators and transition metal dichalcogenides. This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 11974290), the National Key R&D Program of China (Grant No. 2018YFA0306600), and the National Key R&D Program of China (Grant No. 2018YFA0306600). Correspondence: email: yongli@iphy.ac.cn.
Keywords: Optical drag, circular polarization, topological states, graphene.
