We introduce a compact optical setup utilizing multiple gratings to generate the necessary optical fields for the trapping, manipulation, and readout of a single atom. This configuration is designed to be compatible with current single-atom quantum information processors, which typically employ a vacuum chamber equipped with a single access port. We demonstrate that this arrangement can simultaneously generate the optical fields required for vertical trapping, Raman manipulation, and fluorescence readout of a single atom. Furthermore, we show that the system can produce a focused optical dipole trap with a vertical trap depth of 1.2 $\mu$K and a Rayleigh length of 10.5 $\mu$m, while also delivering the excitation and detection beams needed for Raman manipulation and fluorescence readout. This approach offers a compact and cost-effective solution for integrated single-atom trapping, handling, and readout, thereby facilitating the implementation of single-atom quantum information processors. Additionally, we discuss the potential for further miniaturization of the system. Our findings are validated through numerical simulations and experimental results. This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 11974018), the National Key R&D Program of China (Grant No. 2018YFB0704000), and the National Key Research and Development Program of China (Grant No. 2018YFB0704000). Correspondence: email: zhangyong@ustc.edu.cn.
