This study examines the convergence characteristics of distributed logistic regression algorithms. We introduce a novel method that eliminates communication between machines during the initial iterations, demonstrating that this approach matches the convergence rate of the most effective existing algorithms. Furthermore, we establish that the proposed algorithm achieves the optimal convergence rate for all scenarios where the sample size exceeds the number of features. Additionally, we present a new algorithm that removes communication in later iterations and prove that it attains the optimal convergence rate when the number of features exceeds the sample size. Finally, we empirically validate the advantages of our proposed methods. The source code is accessible at \url{https://github.com/ChenYi1997/DC-LLR}.

Keywords: Distributed optimization, Distributed logistic regression, Convergence rate, Distributed gradient descent, Distributed coordinate descent.

